  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/mesarovic-takahara-1975-general-system">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mesarović &amp; Takahara (1975), General Systems Theory: Mathematical Foundations, Ch. II §1(a), Definition 1.1 (book p. 11)</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/mesarovic-takahara-1975-gst">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mesarović, M. D. &amp; Takahara, Y. — General Systems Theory: Mathematical Foundations (Academic Press, 1975)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>Mihajlo D. Mesarović and Yasuhiko Takahara</dcterms:creator>
        <atlas:authoredBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/author/mihajlo-mesarovic"/>
        <atlas:authoredBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/author/yasuhiko-takahara"/>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">1975</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>ISBN 0-12-491540-X</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>Mathematics in Science and Engineering v. 113</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>LCCN 74-1639</dcterms:identifier>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>Chapter II, §1(a), Definition 1.1, book p. 11 = scan page 22; eqs. (2.1)–(2.2)</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>Definition 1.1. A (general) system is a relation on nonempty (abstract) sets
S ⊂ × {Vᵢ : i ∈ I} (2.1)
where × denotes Cartesian product and I is the index set. A component set Vᵢ is referred to as a system object. When I is finite, (2.1) is written in the form
S ⊂ V₁ × ⋯ × Vₙ (2.2)</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>A (general) system is a relation on nonempty (abstract) sets
S ⊂ × {Vᵢ : i ∈ I} (2.1)</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:displayContext>where × denotes Cartesian product and I is the index set. A component set Vᵢ is referred to as a system object.</atlas:displayContext>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/relation">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">relation</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/relation"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/set"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/cartesian-product">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cartesian product</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The operation denoted by × in (2.1) and (2.2), forming the product of the family of sets of which the system is a subset. Role confirmed at the guided pass, 2026-08-26.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/operation"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/index-set">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">index set</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The set I indexing the family of sets Vᵢ over which the product is taken. Role confirmed at the guided pass, 2026-08-26.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/component-set">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">component set</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">A member Vᵢ of the indexed family entering the Cartesian product; the author's word for the factors of the product. Distinct from prim:component (Mobus's word) — merging is a census question. Role confirmed at the guided pass, 2026-08-26.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/system-object">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">system object</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The author's name for a component set Vᵢ once regarded as belonging to the system; introduced by naming, not by further definition. Role confirmed at the guided pass, 2026-08-26.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDHC"/>
    <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py; verbatim checked against the page image by Shingai Thornton 2026-08-26</atlas:encodedBy>
    <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-25</atlas:encodedOn>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">SCOPE OF THE ENTRY. The entry carries Definition 1.1 alone — the set-theoretic definition of a (general) system as a relation on nonempty abstract sets — together with the author's immediate gloss naming × as Cartesian product, I as the index set, and a component set Vᵢ as a system object, plus the finite-index form (2.2). Definitions 1.2 (input object, output object, input-output system), 1.3 (function-type system), and the parallel presentation at book p. 6 (eq. 1.1) are not encoded here.

TRANSCRIPTION. The source file warns that its OCR mangles notation; the verbatim follows the page-image transcription supplied with the source (scan page 22 = book page 11; made 2026-08-25 by a model reading the page image). Verified against the page image by Shingai Thornton, 2026-08-26: ⊂ (proper subset, not ⊆) confirmed in (2.1) and (2.2); "nonempty" confirmed; the gloss sentence confirmed; equation numbers confirmed as printed — and the reviewer caught that (2.1) prints set-builder braces, ×{Vᵢ : i ∈ I}, which the draft had dropped to satisfy the locator. The locator now normalises braces on both sides (reported as a normalisation), and the verbatim carries the braces the page prints. Subscripts are carried as the source file writes them.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED. No cco is-about relation is asserted between this entry and any subject matter, thing, or phenomenon. The catalogue records that this author, in this locus, uses these words in this formal statement; it does not assert what the definition is about, nor that this 'system' is the same notion as any other author's.

WHAT DOES NOT FIT. The passage rules on no examples: it neither offers a concrete thing as a system nor refuses one, so admits and refuses are empty. No qualification is placed on the definition within the passage, so author_caveat is null; the restriction 'The form (2.3) rather than (2.2) will be used throughout this book' belongs to Definition 1.2's neighbourhood and is not carried here. The primitive slugs 'Cartesian product', 'index set', 'component set', and 'system object' are minted new rather than merged with the existing 'component', 'set', or 'object' slugs, because the words the author uses differ; ruled kept-unmerged at the guided pass (2026-08-26) per the census-question rule.

CANDIDATE 0 RULED (2026-08-26, Shingai Thornton). The Ch. I §2 passage at book p. 6 — 'a system is defined as a relation in the set-theoretic sense … S ⊂ × V̄ … We shall primarily be concerned with a system consisting of two objects … S ⊂ X × Y (1.1)' — is a PREVIEW of Definition 1.1, not a distinct definitional claim: Chapter I announces its concepts will be introduced precisely via formalization, the p. 6 statement is the same relation in sketch form, and the author never insists on a distinction between two claims (contrast Bunge, whose general characterisation and definition proper are two entries because he says they differ). Under the catalogue's restatement rule the passage grades PROP, pointing here; it is not accessioned.</rdfs:comment>
    <atlas:floorPosition rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/system-object"/>
    <atlas:floorDependency rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/relation"/>
  </atlas:FormalSystemDefinition>
