  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/joslyn-1995-control2">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Joslyn (1995), Semantic Control Systems, §3.3, Definition 28 (Control₂)</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/joslyn-1995-semantic-control">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Joslyn, C. — Semantic Control Systems (World Futures 45, 1995)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>Cliff Joslyn</dcterms:creator>
        <atlas:authoredBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/author/cliff-joslyn"/>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">1995</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>World Futures 45, pp. 87-123</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1080/02604027.1995.9972556</dcterms:identifier>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>§3.3 Control Theory, Definition 28, p. 103 (from the 2026-08-27 page-image transcription section)</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>Definition 28 (Control₂) A control₁ system CS = ⟨C, O⟩ is also a control₂ system if the variation in the constraint₁ on O, induced by C's variation, is itself constrained₂.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>A control₁ system CS = ⟨C, O⟩ is also a control₂ system if the variation in the constraint₁ on O, induced by C's variation, is itself constrained₂.</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/control-1-system">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">control₁ system</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Joslyn's word for the metasystem CS = ⟨C, O⟩ of Definition 25, in which the actions of the controller C result in a constraint₂ on the freedom of the object of control O; it is the antecedent kind on which control₂ is predicated. [MDU — drafted, unchecked; role proposal: sort]</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/control-2-system">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">control₂ system</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Joslyn's word for the kind of system defined here: a control₁ system whose second-order variation in the constraint₁ on O, induced by C's variation, is itself constrained₂. [MDU — drafted, unchecked; role proposal: sort]</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/constraint-1">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/operation"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">constraint₁</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The author's word, carried into this definition, for the systemic constraint C := X − S of Definition 6; here it is what C places on O and what may itself vary. [MDU — drafted, unchecked; role proposal: operation]</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/constrained-2">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/relation"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">constrained₂</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The author's word, the participial form of constraint₂ (Definition 15, the reduction of some variety), predicated here of the second-order variation in the constraint₁ on O. [MDU — drafted, unchecked; role proposal: relation]</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/variation">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">variation</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The author's word for the varying of a component or of a constraint over its state space; used here at two levels (C's variation, and the variation in the constraint₁ on O) without further formal analysis in the definition itself. [MDU — drafted, unchecked; role proposal: unassigned]</skos:scopeNote>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/thermodynamic-system-with-decoupled-envelope">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Thermodynamic system O in a systematically varying thermal environment C</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>Joslyn's worked example: once O's envelope of variation stays constant while C's temperature changes systematically, the pair CS = ⟨C, O⟩ is declared a control₂ system.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>That is, as Cs temperature changed, O's envelope of variation remained constant. Under these conditions, CS is now a control2 system.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>§3.3, p. 104</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py — UNCHECKED</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-26</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/thermostat-machine">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Thermostat (human-engineered machine)</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>Joslyn offers engineered machines such as thermostats as familiar instances of control₂ systems.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>We are quite familiar with control2 systems which are artifacts, for example human-engineered machines like thermostats.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>§4.6, p. 117</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py — UNCHECKED</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-26</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:admits>
    <atlas:refuses>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/damped-spring-stable-equilibrium">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">System governed only by a stable equilibrium (the damped spring of Eq. 26)</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>Joslyn rules that mere passive stability is not control₂, since varying the free parameters varies the constraint₁ on O and hence the equilibrium point.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>It is clear that a system governed only by a stable equilibrium, as in (26), is not a controlg system: a variation of the free parameters causes a corresponding variation of the constraintj on O, and thus of x*.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>§3.3, p. 104</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py — UNCHECKED</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-26</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:refuses>
    <atlas:refuses>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/boiling-liquid">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Boiling liquid</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>A dynamic equilibrium whose rate tracks the heat input; Joslyn states explicitly that it is not controlled₂.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>The boiling activity is in dynamic equilibrium, but it is not controlled2.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>§3.6, p. 109</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py — UNCHECKED</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-26</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:refuses>
    <atlas:refuses>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/spinning-top">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Spinning top</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>The spin stabilises the axis of revolution, but a tilt of the table moves it; Joslyn notes that a controlled₂ position would resist the perturbation.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>The torque generated by its active spinning creates a stable position of the axis of revolution, but a tilt to the table top will cause that center to move. If the position were controlled2, then it would resist that environmental perturbation.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>§3.6, p. 109</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDU"/>
        <atlas:encodedBy>claude-opus-5 via atlas/ingest/draft_entry.py — UNCHECKED</atlas:encodedBy>
        <atlas:encodedOn rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-26</atlas:encodedOn>
      </atlas:Example>
    </atlas:refuses>
    <atlas:formalisedAs>Systems/Category/ShapeJoslyn.lean#JoslynShape</atlas:formalisedAs>
