  <atlas:FormalSystemDefinition rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/mobus-2022-seven-tuple">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mobus (2022), Systems Science: TAMD, eq. (4.1)</rdfs:label>
    <atlas:statedIn>
      <cco:ont00000253 rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/mobus-2022-tamd">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Mobus, G. E. — Systems Science: Theory, Analysis, Modeling, and Design (Springer, 2022)</rdfs:label>
        <dcterms:creator>George E. Mobus</dcterms:creator>
        <atlas:authoredBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/author/george-mobus"/>
        <dcterms:date rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#gYear">2022</dcterms:date>
        <dcterms:identifier>ISBN 978-3-030-93481-1</dcterms:identifier>
        <dcterms:identifier>doi:10.1007/978-3-030-93482-8</dcterms:identifier>
      </cco:ont00000253>
    </atlas:statedIn>
    <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 4, §4.3.3, eq. (4.1), p. 191</atlas:sourceLocation>
    <atlas:verbatim>Deriving a definition of system from the principles and the ontology, a system S is a 7-tuple:
Sᵢ, ₗ = C, N, G, B, T, H, Δtᵢ, ₗ
where i and l are indexes. The index i is a subsystem index and the index l is the level of organization in the system-subsystem hierarchy. Both are 0 for the initial system of interest, S₀,₀ is the designated SOI.</atlas:verbatim>
    <atlas:displayForm>Sᵢ, ₗ = C, N, G, B, T, H, Δtᵢ, ₗ</atlas:displayForm>
    <atlas:displayContext>where i and l are indexes. The index i is a subsystem index and the index l is the level of organization in the system-subsystem hierarchy. Both are 0 for the initial system of interest, S₀,₀ is the designated SOI.</atlas:displayContext>
    <atlas:authorCaveat>The following definition is proposed as a starting point for developing a formal definition of system.</atlas:authorCaveat>
    <atlas:authorCaveat>There is a point we need to be clear about. What is being presented here is, itself, a concept about what a system consists of, how it is composed. It is not being represented as the "general theory of systems," though it might be a candidate for that title.</atlas:authorCaveat>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/subsystem">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">subsystem</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's index i: 'The index i is a subsystem index.' The definition's own gloss uses the word as a primitive.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/level-of-organization"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/hierarchy"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/system-of-interest">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">system of interest</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's SOI: 'the initial system of interest, S₀,₀ is the designated SOI'. The designated system at which analysis begins, at indexes 0,0.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/constant"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/time"/>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/component">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">component</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's C: 'C is a set of components and their "type" along with membership functions in the event the set is fuzzy, that is, the components may have partial inclusion' (§4.3.3.1).</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/flow-network">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">flow network</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's N: 'N is generally a flow network through which real substances are moving from one node (component) to the next with causal influence' (§4.3.3.2.1).</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/relation"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/bipartite-flow-graph">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">bipartite flow graph</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's G: 'G is a bipartite flow graph defined as:' (§4.3.3.2.2). The prose gives only the name — the definition proper is Eq. 4.5, which follows it.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/relation"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/boundary">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">boundary</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's B: 'The boundary, B in Eq. 4.1, at level l, then is a tuple' (§4.3.3.3). Thin prose, unambiguous word; the tuple's content is Eq. 4.6.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/transformation-rule">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">transformation rule</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's T: 'T is the set of transformation rules for the subsystems in S' (§4.3.3.4). Hand-recovered after the model's wider span failed to locate — the passage is set in display-math wrappers the digitisation mangles.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/operation"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/memory">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">memory</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's H: 'H in very complex systems could be a super complex object that records the history of the system, or its record of state transitions, especially as it develops or evolves' (§4.3.3.5).</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/sort"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:invokesPrimitive>
      <skos:Concept rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/time-interval">
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/PrimitiveScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">time interval</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Mobus's Δt: 'Finally, the last element in S is Δt, a time interval relevant to the level of the system of interest' (§4.3.3.6). The author's own WORD for the element; distinct from the census row prim:time, which records the notational Δt/dt invocations shared across authors.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/role/constant"/>
      </skos:Concept>
    </atlas:invokesPrimitive>
    <atlas:admits>
      <atlas:Example rdf:about="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/mobus-atom">
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">atoms</rdfs:label>
        <atlas:gloss>Atoms, offered as simple systems distinguished by a NULL memory element H.</atlas:gloss>
        <atlas:verbatim>Some simple systems, like atoms for example, may have a NULL H; that is there is no memory of past states and future states depend only on the current state and current inputs.</atlas:verbatim>
        <atlas:sourceLocation>ch. 4, §4.3.3.5</atlas:sourceLocation>
        <atlas:evidenceCode rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/MDHC"/>
